      "completion_tokens": 1339,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure and Polarity\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen is, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side.\n*   **Why this matters:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (a property called **cohesion**) and to other substances (adhesion). It also makes water an excellent **solvent**, meaning it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid.\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nWater behaves in ways that are unusual for a liquid, largely due to those hydrogen bonds between molecules:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve salts, sugars, acids, and gases. This is why it is the main transport medium in plants and animals (blood is mostly water).\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This stabilizes Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body relatively constant.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Most liquids get denser as they freeze, but water is different. When water freezes into ice, its molecules spread out and form a crystalline structure that is less dense than liquid water. Therefore, **ice floats** on liquid water. This is vital for life because it insulates the water below, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid.\n\n### 3. States of Matter\nWater exists in all three states of matter on Earth:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (crystalline structure).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we drink and use for washing.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible in the air).\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant substance on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet. However, 97% of that water is saltwater in the oceans, and only about 3% is fresh water (mostly locked in glaciers and ice caps).\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is the medium in which biochemical reactions occur, it transports nutrients and waste, and it provides the physical structure for cells (cytoplasm). Without water, life as we know it would not exist."
